# Performance Guidelines

## Measure first

- Optimise only with evidence: profile, trace, or benchmark before and after
- Define budgets for what users feel (latency percentiles, page load, memory) and check them in CI where practical
- Fix the biggest bottleneck first; prefer algorithmic and I/O wins over micro-optimisations
- Keep optimised code readable; isolate tricky optimisations behind clear names with a comment explaining why

## Universal techniques

- **Algorithms:** watch for accidental O(n²) (nested loops, repeated linear lookups); use maps/sets for membership
- **I/O:** avoid N+1 queries and chatty calls; batch, paginate, and stream large results
- **Concurrency:** run independent I/O in parallel with bounded concurrency; never block event loops or UI threads with CPU-heavy or synchronous I/O work
- **Caching:** cache idempotent, expensive results with explicit TTL and invalidation; bound cache size; never cache per-user data in shared keys
- **Memory:** bound collections and queues; release listeners, timers, subscriptions, and handles; stream instead of loading whole files
- **Timeouts and backpressure:** every external call has a timeout; shed or queue load instead of collapsing
- **Input rate:** debounce/throttle high-frequency events (search 300–500 ms, resize, scroll)

## Web budgets (reference)

| Metric | Good |
|--------|------|
| LCP (Largest Contentful Paint) | ≤ 2.5 s |
| INP (Interaction to Next Paint) | ≤ 200 ms |
| CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift) | ≤ 0.1 |
| Initial JS (compressed) | Keep small; set a project budget (e.g. ≤ 200 KB) |

INP replaced FID as a Core Web Vital in 2024. Measure with real-user monitoring, not only lab tools.

## Web: loading and bundles

- Code-split by route; lazy-load heavy components (charts, editors, maps) and rarely used features with dynamic `import()`
- Import only what you use; avoid barrel files that defeat tree shaking
- Analyse bundles regularly (e.g. bundle analyzer, `source-map-explorer`); check the size impact of new dependencies
- Lazy-load below-the-fold images (`loading="lazy"`), set explicit dimensions to avoid layout shift, serve modern formats
- Use HTTP caching headers and a CDN for static assets

## Web: rendering (React example)

- Clean up effects: return a cleanup that removes listeners, clears timers, aborts fetches, and disconnects observers
- Virtualise long lists and tables (hundreds of rows or more)
- Memoise only where profiling shows wasted renders (`memo`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`); if the React Compiler is enabled, do not hand-memoise by default
- Keep state close to where it is used; avoid unbounded append-only state
- Move expensive work off the main thread (Web Workers) or defer it (`useDeferredValue`, transitions)

## Desktop (Electron example)

- Keep the main process light; do heavy work in utility processes or workers
- Batch IPC; use `invoke`/`handle` for request-response; avoid large or high-frequency payloads
- Monitor per-process memory; destroy windows that are no longer needed
- Persist and restore window bounds within visible screen area

## Anti-patterns

- Optimising without a measurement
- Queries or network calls inside loops
- Unbounded caches, queues, or arrays
- Effects or subscriptions without cleanup
- Rendering huge lists without virtualisation
- Firing requests on every keystroke

## Checklist

- [ ] Bottleneck identified by measurement; before/after numbers recorded
- [ ] No N+1 queries or calls in loops; large results paginated or streamed
- [ ] External calls have timeouts; concurrency bounded
- [ ] Caches bounded with TTL and invalidation
- [ ] Listeners, timers, and subscriptions cleaned up
- [ ] Web: Core Web Vitals within budget; routes code-split; long lists virtualised
